what exact tribe created

00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:29.305
this thing we know as a johnny cake.

00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:31.075
But it was probably the Shawnee Indians,

00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:32.875
and the Shawnee Native Americans,

00:00:32.875 --> 00:00:35.455
and basically, the
English settlers came in,

00:00:35.455 --> 00:00:38.455
and they are basically mispronouncing it.

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:40.195
So it's supposed to be
called a Shawnee cake,

00:00:40.195 --> 00:00:42.265
but they heard johnny cake.

00:00:42.265 --> 00:00:44.425
So that's where that term comes from.

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:46.495
- And how did hoecake come out of that?

00:00:46.495 --> 00:00:48.505
- So hoe cake was, so enslaved folks,

00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:51.355
when they were brought here 400 years ago,

00:00:51.355 --> 00:00:53.875
they only had access to
cornmeal, water, salt.

00:00:53.875 --> 00:00:55.285
They didn't have heating elements.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:57.025
So they would actually have to make these,

00:00:57.025 --> 00:01:00.145
which is why they're this
shape on the bottom of a hoe,

00:01:00.145 --> 00:01:02.365
of a farming tool, 'cause
that's all they had access to.

00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:04.225
So they would heat their farming tools,

00:01:04.225 --> 00:01:06.445
and they would make their little hoecakes.

00:01:06.445 --> 00:01:07.915
And that's why they're so small.

00:01:07.915 --> 00:01:09.466
Later on, in the 18th and 19th century,

00:01:09.466 --> 00:01:12.203
what we now know today as a griddle,

00:01:12.203 --> 00:01:14.005
